"""Edition 15 original questions and answer-planning frameworks. Run once."""
import pathlib,json,random
R=pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
assert not (R/'data/2026-08-mcqs.json').exists()
rows='''1~The GDP estimate released on 31 August records 7.8% growth. Which interpretation is correct?~Year-on-year real GDP growth in April–June FY2026–27 at constant 2022–23 prices~Month-on-month nominal GDP growth in August|Full-year real GDP growth for FY2026–27|The RBI’s observed August industrial-production rate~The primary release separates real GDP 7.8%, nominal GDP 10.3% and real GVA 8.2%. Publication date is not the reference period, and an official estimate is not a forecast from the earlier MPC meeting.
2~Which set completes the four-sector coverage of the inspected new-series IIP?~Mining; manufacturing; electricity/gas; water supply, sewerage and waste management~Mining; manufacturing; construction; banking|Agriculture; manufacturing; electricity; transport services|Mining; manufacturing; electricity only~The 2022–23-base release gives weights 11.053, 76.062, 10.865 and 2.020, summing to 100. Omitting the fourth sector leaves a real coverage gap, not just rounding.
2~The IIP release mentions June growth of 7.3% and later shows June growth of 8.8%. What is the sound treatment?~Preserve the earlier quick estimate and later final-table vintage as distinct~Average both rates to construct an official final estimate|Label 7.3% as July growth and 8.8% as April–July cumulative growth|Conclude that neither rate can ever be revised~The opening narrative compares against June’s earlier quick estimate; the final sectoral table incorporates updated data. July quick growth is 6.7%; April–July cumulative growth is 6.3%.
3~Consider: 1. The August MPC retained repo at 5.25%. 2. A neutral stance guarantees that the next decision will be a rate cut. Which is correct?~1 only~2 only|Both 1 and 2|Neither 1 nor 2~The primary resolution confirms unchanged rates and a neutral stance. Neutrality preserves flexibility; it is not forward commitment to a cut or a guarantee about every commercial bank’s lending rate.
4~Which sequence best preserves the legal stages of an ordinary parliamentary Bill?~Passage by both Houses, assent, then commencement according to the applicable provisions~Committee referral, automatic commencement, then assent|Introduction, automatic assent, then optional passage|Cabinet approval, judicial approval, then automatic commencement~The reviewed session’s 11 passed Bills are not independently verified as 11 commenced Acts. Some Acts commence on assent; others use specified dates or later notifications, so commencement must be checked.
6~Which distinction is correct in the MSME/TReDS context?~Invoice registration and financing are distinct from final buyer payment~TReDS registration cancels the buyer’s liability|A mediation timeline proves all disputes were actually resolved|Every discounted invoice is a government capital grant~Receivables financing can address liquidity, but buyer acceptance, payment discipline and dispute settlement remain important. A proposed or statutory mechanism should not be confused with actual performance.
8~What does the reviewed BHAVYA–Rasayan approval establish?~A national support framework for three greenfield parks, not verified selection of an AP location~Completion of three operational parks in Andhra Pradesh|An unlimited central grant for every chemical enterprise|A waiver of all environmental appraisal for common infrastructure~PRS records ₹3,030 crore over FY2026–27 to FY2030–31, with central support capped at ₹1,000 crore per park and state participation. Approval does not certify site selection, commissioning or compliance.
9~Why distinguish Exporter of Record from Seller of Record?~They can carry different compliance and commercial responsibilities under the reviewed framework~Only the seller can ever be responsible for customs filings|The distinction exempts every online transaction from foreign-exchange rules|It makes returned export goods automatically domestic sales~The reviewed arrangement separates EOR and SOR, addresses confirmed orders, seller payments and returns, and does not eliminate customs or other regulatory obligations.
13~Which statement about the reviewed SHANTI rules is defensible?~Composite licensing can coexist with phase-specific safety authorisations, but the reviewed rules were drafts~A composite licence automatically proves commercial nuclear operation|A comment deadline establishes that the draft is already final law|Every proposed licence transfers nuclear safety oversight to a private operator~PRS describes consultation-stage rules and phase-specific AERB authorisations. Licensing, construction, safety approval and operation remain distinct milestones.
14~A CBG project has eligible installed capacity of 5 tonnes per day. At a ceiling of ₹2 crore per TPD, what is the corresponding maximum capacity-linked assistance before other eligibility conditions?~₹10 crore~₹2 crore per year for five years automatically|₹10 crore for every tonne of fuel actually sold|₹23,731 crore~5 TPD × ₹2 crore/TPD = ₹10 crore. This is a ceiling illustration, not a sanction. Capacity-linked capital assistance is different from the administered energy price of ₹2,110/MMBTU.
14~Which is NOT established by the GOBARdhan approval alone?~That nearly ten-fold production growth has already been achieved~That a multi-year scheme outlay was approved|That assured offtake is part of scheme design|That pipeline infrastructure and credit support are included~The ₹23,731 crore approval runs from FY2026–27 to FY2035–36. The release’s production expansion and investment outcomes are objectives, not an independently audited realised result.
15~The 200 SCM incentive in the inspected domestic PNG scheme is allocated to whom and on what basis?~Eligible CGD entities per incremental billed domestic connection above a GA threshold~Every household as 200 SCM of free gas each month|Every LNG importer per tonne of gas imported|Only state electricity utilities per new power connection~The company-level allocation substitutes costlier gas sourcing and is intended to improve connection economics. Approval is 18 August, with effectiveness from 1 September; it is not a universal household free-gas benefit.
16~Why is a tribunals commission not by itself sufficient to guarantee independent adjudication?~Appointments, tenure, resources and safeguards against executive dominance also matter~Tribunals cannot use technical experts under any circumstances|Commission creation automatically removes constitutional judicial review|All tribunal vacancies are necessarily filled on the date of a Bill’s passage~Institutional form and actual independence differ. The reviewed Bill’s appointment provisions do not establish operational capacity or eliminate the supervisory role of constitutional courts.
17~Which combination correctly describes the reviewed ISI proposal?~Pending Bill; proposed body-corporate not-for-profit framework~Commenced Act; mandatory conversion into a listed profit-making company|Supreme Court judgment; closure of all research programmes|State-only executive order; no parliamentary proposal~The 3 August Bill remained pending in the reviewed session. Corporate legal personality does not necessarily mean a profit-making private company or completed transformation.
19~The conventional-missile ToT approval should be read as what?~Permission for industrial production subject to applicable qualifications, certifications and regulation~Automatic export clearance for every missile to every country|Proof all approved systems have already entered mass production|Transfer of all military operational command to industry~The 25 August primary release links technology transfer to domestic capability and MSME participation. Technology absorption, qualified production and export permission are different stages.
21~Which treatment of the telecom-identification evidence is accurate?~Commencement on publication is directly checked; later detailed KYC provisions were followed through PRS~Every clause of the eight-chunk Gazette was independently read|The inspected document was only an unnotified consultation draft|Aadhaar establishes a subscriber’s citizenship~The opening Gazette passage confirms the 21 August notification and commencement. Source provenance must not exaggerate how much of the primary instrument was inspected; identity, citizenship and authentication remain distinct.
24~The rural-employment audit document includes a proposed commencement clause. Does that make it final operative rules?~No; the containing Gazette notice publishes draft rules for consultation~Yes; every clause inside any Gazette publication is final law|Yes; the comment period applies only after automatic nationwide implementation|No; draft rules can never contain commencement wording~The 7 August preamble is a consultation notice. Annual financial audit and at least six-monthly social audit are proposed arrangements, not proof that the final instrument was notified.
26~What does a digitised parcel map establish by itself?~A mapped record, not necessarily conclusive and dispute-free title~An automatic court decree resolving all competing claims|Automatic approval of every bank loan application|Transfer of every common land parcel to the mapped occupier~Survey, mutation, registration and title adjudication have distinct legal roles. Public notice, correction and appeal are essential where records or boundaries are contested.
30~The US$5 billion India–Uzbekistan trade figure in the reviewed visit is what?~A bilateral target for 2030, not verified already realised annual trade~A completed Indian budget grant to Uzbekistan|The exact value of trade in August alone|A compulsory payment under a new customs union~PRS supplies the substantive visit summary. The attempted MEA outcomes page returned navigation only, so no full primary verification is claimed. Partnership targets depend on connectivity and commercial follow-through.
33~Which is a component of non-revenue water that need NOT involve physical leakage?~Authorised unbilled consumption~Water lost from a burst pipe|Water overflowing a storage tank|Water leaking from a valve~NRW includes real losses, apparent losses and authorised unbilled use. Different components need different technical, metering and billing responses; treating all NRW as leakage misdiagnoses the problem.
35~For otherwise comparable bonds, what does a greenium generally imply?~Lower green-bond yield and correspondingly higher price~Higher green-bond yield and necessarily lower price|Guaranteed greater emissions reduction regardless of use of proceeds|Identical liquidity and risk for every issuer~Price and yield move inversely. A greenium does not itself prove environmental additionality; comparable maturity, liquidity and credible allocation/reporting still matter.
36~Can a glacier become thinner while moving more slowly?~Yes; thinning can reduce driving stress and ice flow~No; slower flow always proves recovery|No; flow speed and mass balance are identical variables|Yes, but only if all downstream river flow immediately stops~The reviewed Zanskar mechanism illustrates that movement, mass balance and runoff differ. Response varies by geometry and debris cover; a precise basin-wide peak-water date was not independently verified.
37~Which observation alone is insufficient to establish a cloudburst?~100 mm rainfall accumulated over an entire day~At least 100 mm within an hour over a small local area|A suitably documented hourly extreme meeting IMD’s threshold|A time-resolved local record consistent with the threshold~Daily accumulation does not establish the required hourly intensity. Flood damage alone also cannot identify a cloudburst; exposure, drainage and catchment saturation matter.
40~Which statement best distinguishes AI-assisted biological design from an approved therapy?~A computational design still requires synthesis, testing and biosafety/clinical validation~A generated genome sequence is automatically a safe medicine|Every bacteriophage infects human cells rather than bacteria|No laboratory work is necessary after a model generates DNA~Generative tools propose biological designs. Viability, safety, efficacy and regulatory approval are distinct stages, and exact experimental counts were not independently verified in this batch.
44~Which pairing is correct for the inspected 2026 Dirac Medal announcement?~Deepak Dhar — statistical mechanics and sandpile-model contributions~Deepak Dhar — sole winner for discovery of a new elementary particle|Marc Mézard — a medal awarded exclusively by ISRO|Haim Sompolinsky — a 2026 Nobel Prize announced by ICTP~ICTP names four recipients: Derrida, Dhar, Mézard and Sompolinsky. The award recognises theoretical-physics contributions; it is not a Nobel Prize or a sole-recipient award.
45~Which assertion about lab-grown diamonds requires correction?~They necessarily possess the same geological rarity as mined diamonds~They have diamond’s crystalline carbon structure|HPHT and CVD are major production routes|Energy sources influence lifecycle emissions~Laboratory-grown diamonds are real diamonds, but origin, geological scarcity and market value can differ. Ethical traceability and low-carbon production require evidence rather than a label alone.
50~What is the best interpretation of AP’s reported ₹1,500 crore PPP Bridge/VGF fund?~A draft-policy support provision requiring later appraisal and implementation checks~Audited expenditure already completed on every PPP project|Proof that infrastructure creates no future public liability|A universal grant payable to every private developer~The 6 August Cabinet report concerns draft-policy approval. Tendering, financial closure, project operation and public fiscal exposure require separate scrutiny; original GOs were not read.
51~Which legal distinction matters in reporting the Disha decision?~Cabinet approval to withdraw a Bill is not automatically repeal of a commenced Act~Every Cabinet decision immediately repeals all conflicting criminal law|A withdrawn Bill must already have completed assent and commencement|Withdrawal of one proposal removes all existing legal protections~The report describes a Bill-withdrawal approval. Further legislative action and the operative legal framework require verification; women’s safety also depends on institutions and applicable existing law.
57~Which calculation is consistent with the Netanna Sevalo report?~71,536 families × ₹25,000 = ₹178.84 crore, rounded to about ₹179 crore~71,536 families × ₹25,000 = ₹17.884 crore|₹179 crore proves every household in AP received ₹25,000|The 62,541 electricity-support families must be identical to the Netanna count~Different schemes have different units and eligibility. A reported launch and release are not an independent audit of every account credit or proof that all eligible families were included.
61~Which statement correctly interprets the elephant-conservation release?~₹500 crore concerns the combined southern four-state plan; 13 August is used as a reporting date~₹500 crore was verified as exclusively spent in Andhra Pradesh|The source proves every corridor was fully restored by 12 August|The release establishes an identical event date despite its conflicting posting/day wording~The plan covers Karnataka, Kerala, AP and Tamil Nadu. PIB is posted 13 August and says today while World Elephant Day is 12 August, so the note preserves reporting-date precision and does not infer completion.'''
# References in the final four AP questions are checked against authoritative IDs below.
rows=rows.replace('57~Which calculation','57~Which calculation')
positions=[0]*8+[1]*8+[2]*7+[3]*7;random.Random(15082026).shuffle(positions)
Q=[]
for i,line in enumerate(rows.splitlines(),1):
 ref,question,correct,other,explanation=line.split('~');d=other.split('|');random.Random(1500+i).shuffle(d);pos=positions[i-1];d.insert(pos,correct)
 Q.append(dict(id=f'AUG26-{i:02}',question=question,options=d,answer=pos,explanation=explanation,ref=f'E15-2608-{int(ref):03}'))
assert len(Q)==30
(R/'data/2026-08-mcqs.json').write_text(json.dumps(Q,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
F=[
(4,'Legislative productivity should be evaluated through deliberation as well as output.',[
'Anchor the reviewed 20 July–13 August session: 12 introduced, 11 passed, low scheduled-time use. Explain that these counts do not certify final commencement.',
'Distinguish introduction, committee referral, passage, assent and commencement; use the pending ISI Bill as an institutional-stage example.',
'Assess debate quality, committee evidence, fiscal scrutiny, stakeholder input and post-legislative review rather than a single passage total.',
'Balance timely lawmaking with adequate deliberation; propose better scheduling, published ministerial responses and scrutiny of delegated rules.',
'For APPSC, compare state legislative accountability without assuming identical procedures or inventing subsequent Assembly outcomes.'
]),
(14,'Circular bioenergy requires an integrated value chain, not only capital subsidy.',[
'Anchor the 6 August approval: ₹23,731 crore, FY2026–27 to FY2035–36. Production expansion is a target, not already realised output.',
'Map feedstock aggregation, digestion/upgrading, gas quality, pipeline evacuation/offtake, digestate value and reliable payments.',
'Explain the six support components and distinguish ₹2,110/MMBTU pricing from assistance up to ₹2 crore/TPD of installed capacity.',
'Examine methane leakage, water demand, local competing biomass uses, seasonal supply and lifecycle emissions; subsidy cannot cure a weak operating model.',
'For AP, propose district resource mapping and transparent monitoring of actual offtake, farmer income and environmental performance; no specific state allocation is verified.'
]),
(21,'Digital identity systems should make security and inclusion mutually reinforcing.',[
'Anchor the 21 August telecom notification, stating that opening Gazette status was checked while detailed later KYC provisions rely on PRS.',
'Distinguish identity registration, authentication, service access and citizenship; biometrics are not infallible and need privacy safeguards.',
'Analyse fraud prevention alongside biometric failure, disability, documentation gaps, connectivity and point-of-sale capability.',
'Propose accessible alternatives, assisted correction, data minimisation, secure retention, accountable vendors and meaningful grievance redress.',
'For AP tribal and rural users, measure genuine service continuity and fraud reduction rather than only completed verifications; do not assume all old subscribers lose access.'
]),
(24,'Social audit is effective only when findings lead to protected, reasoned follow-up.',[
'Anchor the 7 August DRAFT consultation. Proposed commencement language inside a draft does not prove that final rules are operational.',
'Explain annual financial audit versus at least six-monthly community social audit; map the independent unit, Gram Sabha and responsible officials.',
'Connect worker records, actual receipts and physical assets through accessible publication and evidence-based hearings; do not presume every mismatch is fraud.',
'Assess independence, retaliation risk, action-taken reporting, recoveries, appeals and data protection. Identify later deadlines as PRS-reviewed proposed clauses.',
'For AP employment-transition cases, maintain liability/used-days records and payment correction; measure resolved grievances and worker receipt rather than meetings alone.'
]),
(40,'AI-enabled biological design demands proportionate dual-use governance.',[
'Anchor selected magazine discussion without asserting an independently verified August discovery date or precise experimental success count.',
'Distinguish sequence generation, synthesis, biological viability, safety, clinical efficacy and approval. Explain bacteriophages and AMR potential.',
'Analyse capability amplification, sequence misuse, ecological uncertainty and insecure biological datasets alongside legitimate research benefits.',
'Propose risk-based access, sequence screening, laboratory biosafety, expert review, incident reporting and responsible disclosure; avoid blanket technological determinism.',
'For India and AP research ecosystems, build skills, secure infrastructure and credible institutions; evaluate validated benefit and safety rather than model benchmark hype.'
]),
(50,'Public–private partnerships require transparent allocation of fiscal and service risks.',[
'Anchor AP Cabinet draft-policy approval on 6 August and the reported ₹1,500 crore Bridge/VGF provision, not completed expenditure.',
'Distinguish fund ceiling, viability support, guarantee, loan, tender award, financial closure and operation; map contingent public liabilities.',
'Assess demand forecasts, affordable tariffs, land and environmental compliance, competition, contract disclosure and renegotiation rules.',
'Link national road-concession lessons to AP without treating a model agreement as a signed state contract. Allocate risk to the party best able to manage it.',
'Conclude with service-quality monitoring, independent audits and long-term fiscal disclosure; count performing assets and public value, not headline project totals.'
]),
(57,'Weaver welfare should combine income support with a competitive production ecosystem.',[
'Anchor 7 August Chirala launch as reported on 8 August: 71,536 families, ₹25,000 each, ₹178.84 crore rounded to ₹179 crore.',
'Distinguish launch, reported release, verified receipt and eligibility; separate the family count from different electricity-support denominators.',
'Assess yarn costs, working capital, power, design, authenticity, cooperatives, digital/export access and buyer payment discipline.',
'Link local tourism and handloom marketing while avoiding claims of already realised agreement-based sales or universal benefits.',
'For AP, propose assisted grievance correction and outcome monitoring: household income, sustainable orders and youth participation alongside reliable welfare delivery.'
]),
(61,'Human–elephant coexistence requires landscape governance across administrative borders.',[
'Anchor the PIB release posted 13 August; preserve reporting-date precision. The ₹500 crore plan covers four southern states, not AP alone.',
'Map corridor connectivity, habitat restoration, crop protection, early warning, rapid response and community participation.',
'Connect HANUMAN field capacity with veterinary care, rail safety and safe electricity infrastructure; technology requires trained staff and validation.',
'Assess timely assistance, transparent incident investigation, humane response and interstate protocols without assuming all plan components are installed.',
'For AP, track human safety, animal welfare, corridor function and response time; a plan launch or award does not prove a decline in conflict deaths.'
])]
lines=['# August 2026 · Eight original Mains frameworks','','Original answer-planning aids, not official model answers or predictions. Preserve historical dates, legal stages and source limits. Each framework can be adapted to a 150- or 250-word answer.']
for ref,title,points in F:
 lines+=['','## '+title,'','**Study reference:** '+f'E15-2608-{ref:03}','']+[f'{i}. {x}' for i,x in enumerate(points,1)]+['','**Conclusion test:** Name the responsible institution, safeguard and measurable outcome; distinguish a proposal from implementation.']
(R/'volumes/August-2026-Mains-Frameworks.md').write_text('\n'.join(lines)+'\n')
print('Created 30 August MCQs and 8 Mains frameworks; answer positions 8 A / 8 B / 7 C / 7 D')
